WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:07.230
Tony, a fisherman, has been living in a house
by the lake since he was young.

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:12.599
Over the years, the lake is becoming more
popular and one day a large hotel gets built.

00:00:12.599 --> 00:00:17.250
The hotel obscures all the sunlight and the
guests leave their trash on the beach . To

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:20.150
Tony these are negative externalities.

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:25.060
Although, the good smell of the freshly roasted
coffee from the coffee shop that opened right

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:28.439
next to the hotel is a positive externality
though

00:00:28.439 --> 00:00:32.660
Things start getting complicated when a paper
factory begins polluting the river that flows

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:36.960
down the mountain into the lake where Tony
and other fishermen earn a living.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:39.760
Can you think of the effects the factory may
have on the community?

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:45.390
You may pause the video and share your thoughts
before we continue!

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:48.860
The paper factory is using the clean water
from the mountain river.

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:52.940
Through the production process, the water
gets dirty and afterwards the polluted water

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:54.980
is put back into the river.

00:00:54.980 --> 00:01:00.090
This water then kills a lot of fish downstream
- a cost to society that no one covers.

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:03.740
Tony and the fishermen start to fear for their
existence.

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:07.840
And then things get worse…

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:12.030
The paper mill, which doesn’t factor the
cost of the dead fish into their cost of production,

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:15.340
is therefore charging too little when selling
the paper.

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:20.229
As a consequence, the paper is so cheap that
the coffee shop starts using disposable cups

00:01:20.229 --> 00:01:22.500
where they were using porcelain before.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:27.400
This again leads to more production of paper,
more polluted water, more fish dying and more

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.000
trash on the beach.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:33.070
Tony is getting anxious.

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:38.000
To change the situation, the fishermen decide
to stage public protests until the local government

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.580
announces a new law.

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:44.120
The factory is now forced to fully internalize
the costs of their waste management through

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:45.900
the installation of filters.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:50.460
Soon after, the water is clean again and Tony
and the other fishermen can go back to selling

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:52.510
the same amount of fish they used to.

00:01:52.510 --> 00:01:55.820
And since the price of the paper goes up to
cover the costs of the filtration systems,

00:01:55.820 --> 00:02:01.020
the coffee shops begin serving drinks in real
cups again.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:05.939
We can illustrate what is happening on a supply
and demand chart with price and quantity demanded

00:02:05.939 --> 00:02:06.939
for the paper:

00:02:06.939 --> 00:02:12.810
In an unregulated market, the factory supplies
its products based on so-called private costs,

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:16.560
such as construction, salaries and the raw
materials.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.390
Demand meets supply accordingly.

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:22.530
If the paper factory would have added the
costs of the damages that it causes to society,

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:26.090
the supply curve would have shifted to the
left, leading to an equilibrium reflecting

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:29.030
the true costs, including the social costs.

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:36.069
Now the price of paper is higher and the quantity
of paper demanded is lower.

00:02:36.069 --> 00:02:39.569
Pollution and other negative externalities
are especially prevalent where people lack

00:02:39.569 --> 00:02:43.030
the same moral values and where things tend
to be free of charge.

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:47.470
Such is the case with water or air.

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:50.130
Fishing at high seas is such a case.

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:54.390
Since no one can fully control the oceans
and the high sea doesn’t really belong to

00:02:54.390 --> 00:03:02.460
anyone, everyone takes as much as they can
- a phenomenon called the tragedy of the commons.

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:07.750
To combat negative externalities and avoid
such a tragedy, we can do four things: create

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:14.280
public awareness, assign property rights appropriately,
force the internalization of negative externalities

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:18.960
or turn to regulation and fines.

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:20.900
Let's summarize:

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:24.959
Externalities are consequences of an activity
experienced by a third party.

00:03:24.959 --> 00:03:29.770
Negative externalities that are not internalized,
distort supply and demand at the expense of

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:30.950
third parties.

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:36.600
To avoid market distortion and a tragedy of
the commons, we can combat negative externalities.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:42.320
Ready to help us think how to do that in order
to curb CO2 emissions?

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:47.310
People that drive cars that run on petrol
cause emissions, including CO2 which is responsible

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:49.530
for air pollution and climate change.

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:53.950
In order to reduce emissions, some governments
give their citizens a financial incentive

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:55.700
if they buy an electric car.

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:59.000
Imagine you are the Minister of Transportation
in your country.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:03.060
Could you think of another way to increase
the number of electric cars on the streets

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:05.450
by applying the concept of externalities?

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:23.280
Please write down your ideas in the comments
below!

